From 9f46b5ebfb1ed31066bf550ea6c569c297eef7bf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: qiyinxi <qiyinxi@foxmail.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 10:56:05 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 28/42] host: stop waiting on a dead guest process's fences and
 semaphores

A guest process that is killed while it waits in vkWaitForFences or
vkWaitSemaphores with a long or infinite timeout leaves its render
thread blocked in the host driver. FrameBuffer::cleanupProcGLObjects()
waits until the process has no render thread left, and it runs on the
renderer's only cleanup thread, so that one wait blocks the cleanup of
every later process, and RendererImpl::stop() blocks joining it
(ModAVD/ModAVD#40).

Do what upstream gfxstream c1cecd29 does, without the process resource
refactoring it builds on:

- ProcessResources gets setGuestProcessDead() / isGuestProcessDead().
  RendererImpl::cleanupProcGLObjects() sets it before queueing the
  cleanup.
- RenderThread::main() hands the process resources to
  RenderThreadInfoVk. The cleanup task owns them until the process has
  no render thread left, which is the same lifetime the seqno check in
  VkDecoder already relies on. A cleanup task that the cleanup worker
  drops because it is stopping (renderer shutdown) used to free them
  while that process's render threads could still read them; it now
  leaks them instead.
- vkWaitForFences and vkWaitSemaphores wait in steps of at most 1 s and
  return VK_TIMEOUT once the process is dead, with a warning. Finite
  timeouts are charged with the time that passed, on steady_clock.
  UINT64_MAX stays infinite instead of becoming UINT64_MAX minus the
  time spent waiting for the fence to be submitted.

The loop lives in vk_wait_utils.h with an injectable clock, so it is
tested without a GPU. The wiring around it (the flag, the pointer in
RenderThreadInfoVk) is not unit tested; a guest program that is killed
in such a wait covers it.

Test: VkWaitUtilsTest.* 12/12 (with the rest of Vulkan_synctest, 40/40);
      each of 4 mutations of vk_wait_utils.h fails at least one case
Test: a guest program killed while waiting with UINT64_MAX in
      vkWaitForFences, in vkWaitSemaphores, or in vkWaitForFences while
      a second thread polls vkGetFenceStatus: with this change and the
      next, the process was cleaned up in 16/16 runs, the longest wait
      for its render threads 1028 ms. Without them there was no cleanup,
      and about 30 s later the emulator aborted on hanging threads.
Test: Star Rail and Endfield force-stopped 10 times each while loading
      and running: cleaned up as before (Endfield 9/10; the first stop
      came before the game used the GPU), and the game started again
      each time

Refs ModAVD/ModAVD#40
